<#
================================================================================
 QueryExecutionHistory.ps1
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 A WinForms GUI for viewing recent query execution history on a SQL Server
 instance -- a lightweight, Query-Store-aware alternative to hand-writing
 DMV queries every time someone asks "what's been running / why is proc X
 slow right now".

 WHAT IT DOES
   - You point it at a Server + Database.
   - It auto-detects whether Query Store is ON for that database and
     transparently uses the right backend:
         Query Store ON  -> sys.query_store_* catalog views (durable history)
         Query Store OFF -> plan cache DMVs (sys.dm_exec_query_stats /
                             sys.dm_exec_procedure_stats) as a best-effort
                             fallback -- see the caveats in the "No Query
                             Store" section below.
   - Time range defaults to the LAST 30 MINUTES, with quick presets and a
     custom range option.
   - Search box accepts EITHER a Query Store query_id (a plain integer) OR
     a stored procedure name (schema.name). When Query Store is off, a
     hex value (0x....) is treated as a plan-cache query_hash instead,
     since plan cache has no persistent query_id.
   - PSP-aware (SQL Server 2022+ Parameter Sensitive Plan optimization):
     searching a PSP-optimized proc by name/query_id automatically expands
     to its query variants via sys.query_store_query_variant, because the
     "dispatcher" query_id you'd find directly has no plans/runtime stats
     of its own -- those live under separate variant query_ids that report
     object_id = 0. Without this, such a proc would search-return nothing.
     Rows resulting from this expansion are flagged is_psp_variant = 1 with
     psp_dispatcher_query_id set, and proc_name is still resolved via the
     dispatcher. No-op (and no error) on pre-2022 instances.
   - Designed to stay fast regardless of user-data size (see PERFORMANCE
     NOTES below) -- it never reads a single row of your actual tables.
   - A Chart tab sits next to the results grid: one colored bar per query
     (like Query Store's own "Top Resource Consuming Queries" view), with
     a metric dropdown (duration / CPU / executions / logical reads) and
     a hover tooltip per bar. It's built from whichever backend answered
     the search, so it looks and behaves the same whether Query Store is
     on or you're on the plan-cache fallback.
   - A "Remove Plan..." button (next to Search) lets you discard a plan
     found by the last search: sp_query_store_remove_plan when Query
     Store is on, DBCC FREEPROCCACHE(plan_handle) when it's off. If the
     search turned up more than one plan for the query, you're prompted
     to pick which one; with only one, it goes straight to a confirm
     dialog. THIS IS THE ONE PART OF THE TOOL THAT WRITES ANYTHING -- it
     forces a recompile of whatever you remove, cannot be undone, and
     needs elevated permission (db_owner for the Query Store path,
     ALTER SERVER STATE for the DBCC path) beyond what read-only search
     needs. Everything else in this tool only ever issues SELECT.
   - A "Force Plan..." button (Query Store only -- stays disabled on the
     plan-cache fallback, since plan cache has no such concept) pins the
     plan you pick for its query via sp_query_store_force_plan. Click it
     again on an already-forced plan and it offers to unforce instead
     (sp_query_store_unforce_plan) -- also needs db_owner.
   - The search box remembers what you've searched: it's a ComboBox now,
     not a plain textbox, backed by a small JSON file next to this script
     (QueryExecutionHistory.history.json) that's loaded on startup and
     updated after every successful search. Click the dropdown arrow to
     reuse a past query_id/proc name, or just start typing for
     autocomplete against your own history.

 REQUIREMENTS
   - Windows PowerShell 5.1 (uses the in-box System.Data.SqlClient; this is
     what ships with every Windows Server/desktop, so there is nothing to
     install). PowerShell 7 works too IF System.Data.SqlClient or
     Microsoft.Data.SqlClient is resolvable in that session.
   - A login with VIEW SERVER STATE (for the plan-cache path) and/or
     VIEW DATABASE STATE / db_owner (for Query Store views, needed to read
     sys.query_store_*) on the target database.
   - Searching is READ-ONLY: it issues SELECT statements against system
     catalog views/DMVs only, never touches user tables, never writes
     anything. The one exception is the opt-in "Remove Plan..." button
     (see below) -- that's a deliberate, confirmed, one-row action, not
     something a search ever triggers on its own.

 PERFORMANCE NOTES (why this stays fast even on a 40 TB database)
   - Every query here reads Query Store's or the plan cache's internal
     METADATA ONLY. That metadata's size depends on query diversity and
     retention settings -- NOT on the size of your user data. A 40 TB
     OLTP database and a 40 GB database with a similar workload shape
     perform identically here.
   - The Query Store query filters sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval
     (small: one row per retention interval, e.g. hourly) BEFORE joining
     to the much larger runtime_stats table -- filter-the-small-table-first.
   - Every query uses OPTION (RECOMPILE). These are deliberately written as
     "catch-all" queries (query_id OR proc name OR neither), and RECOMPILE
     lets the optimizer build a plan tuned to whichever parameter was
     actually supplied on each run, instead of risking a plan cached for
     a different search that performs badly for this one. Recompiling
     against small metadata tables is cheap; parameter-sniffing bugs on a
     catch-all query are not.
   - Every result set is capped (TOP N, configurable, default 500 rows) so
     a broad "no filter" search can't accidentally pull back everything.

 NO QUERY STORE? READ THIS
   The plan cache is VOLATILE -- entries are evicted on recompiles, memory
   pressure, index/statistics changes, or a service restart. This view
   shows "what's still cached", not a durable log. A query that ran inside
   your time window but was since evicted will not appear. If you need a
   real, durable execution history without enabling Query Store, the right
   long-term answer is a lightweight Extended Events session
   (rpc_completed / sql_batch_completed) writing to a rollover file target
   -- that is a natural phase 2 for this tool, not built here yet.

 USAGE
   powershell.exe -File .\QueryExecutionHistory.ps1
   clear 
cd D:\DBALearning
.\QueryExecutionHistory_v3_planremove.ps1
================================================================================
#>

[CmdletBinding()]
param()

#region ------------------------- Assemblies ---------------------------------

Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Windows.Forms
Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Drawing
Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Data

# The Chart control ships in-box with .NET Framework (Windows PowerShell
# 5.1), which is why this whole tool avoids extra installs. It is NOT
# in-box on PowerShell 7 / .NET (Core), so this is wrapped: if it can't
# load, the app still runs fine with the grid alone, just without the
# Chart tab.
$script:ChartAvailable = $true
try { Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Windows.Forms.DataVisualization -ErrorAction Stop }
catch { $script:ChartAvailable = $false }

[System.Windows.Forms.Application]::EnableVisualStyles()

#endregion

#region ------------------------------ SQL text -------------------------------

# Query Store path. Parameters bound: @QueryId (int, nullable), @ProcName
# (nvarchar, nullable), @StartUtc / @EndUtc (datetimeoffset), @RowCap (int).
#
# PSP (Parameter Sensitive Plan optimization, SQL Server 2022+) AWARENESS:
# When PSP splits a query into per-parameter-range variants, the query row
# you find by object_id/query_id is the "dispatcher" -- it never itself
# executes and carries NO rows in query_store_plan/runtime_stats. The real
# plans live under separate "variant" query_ids (sys.query_store_query_variant
# .query_variant_query_id), and those variant rows report object_id = 0 in
# sys.query_store_query (same as an ad-hoc query), so a plain object_id/
# query_id filter silently returns zero rows for any PSP-optimized proc.
# #TargetQueryIds below resolves direct matches AND (when available) their
# variants, so PSP procs behave the same as any other search.
# sys.query_store_query_variant only exists on SQL Server 2022+ (and Azure
# SQL). OBJECT_ID() is a safe metadata lookup on any version (it does not
# require the object to exist), but a direct static reference to the view
# would fail to bind at compile time on older versions even inside a
# never-taken branch -- so the variant lookup runs as dynamic SQL, guarded
# by that OBJECT_ID() check, targeting the #temp table (visible to dynamic
# SQL in the same session, unlike a table variable).
$script:SqlQueryStore = @'
DECLARE @ObjectId INT = CASE WHEN @ProcName IS NOT NULL THEN OBJECT_ID(@ProcName) END;

IF @ProcName IS NOT NULL AND @ObjectId IS NULL
BEGIN
    RAISERROR(N'Could not resolve proc name "%s" to an object in this database.', 16, 1, @ProcName);
    RETURN;
END;

CREATE TABLE #TargetQueryIds (query_id BIGINT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, dispatcher_query_id BIGINT NULL);

INSERT INTO #TargetQueryIds (query_id, dispatcher_query_id)
SELECT q.query_id, NULL
FROM sys.query_store_query AS q
WHERE (@QueryId  IS NULL OR q.query_id  = @QueryId)
  AND (@ObjectId IS NULL OR q.object_id = @ObjectId);

IF OBJECT_ID('sys.query_store_query_variant') IS NOT NULL
BEGIN
    DECLARE @PspSql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'
INSERT INTO #TargetQueryIds (query_id, dispatcher_query_id)
SELECT qv.query_variant_query_id, qv.parent_query_id
FROM sys.query_store_query_variant AS qv
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM #TargetQueryIds t WHERE t.query_id = qv.parent_query_id)
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM #TargetQueryIds t2 WHERE t2.query_id = qv.query_variant_query_id);';
    EXEC sys.sp_executesql @PspSql;
END;

;WITH Ivl AS (
    SELECT runtime_stats_interval_id
    FROM sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval
    WHERE start_time < @EndUtc AND end_time > @StartUtc
),
Agg AS (
    SELECT
        rs.plan_id,
        SUM(rs.count_executions) AS count_executions,
        SUM(CAST(rs.count_executions AS DECIMAL(19,4)) * rs.avg_duration)        / NULLIF(SUM(rs.count_executions),0) AS avg_duration_us,
        MAX(rs.max_duration) AS max_duration_us,
        MIN(rs.min_duration) AS min_duration_us,
        SUM(CAST(rs.count_executions AS DECIMAL(19,4)) * rs.avg_cpu_time)        / NULLIF(SUM(rs.count_executions),0) AS avg_cpu_us,
        SUM(CAST(rs.count_executions AS DECIMAL(19,4)) * rs.avg_logical_io_reads) / NULLIF(SUM(rs.count_executions),0) AS avg_logical_reads,
        SUM(CAST(rs.count_executions AS DECIMAL(19,4)) * rs.avg_physical_io_reads)/ NULLIF(SUM(rs.count_executions),0) AS avg_physical_reads,
        SUM(CAST(rs.count_executions AS DECIMAL(19,4)) * rs.avg_rowcount)        / NULLIF(SUM(rs.count_executions),0) AS avg_rowcount,
        SUM(CASE WHEN rs.execution_type <> 0 THEN rs.count_executions ELSE 0 END) AS non_regular_count
    FROM sys.query_store_runtime_stats AS rs
    JOIN Ivl ON Ivl.runtime_stats_interval_id = rs.runtime_stats_interval_id
    WHERE rs.plan_id IN (SELECT p2.plan_id FROM sys.query_store_plan AS p2 WHERE p2.query_id IN (SELECT query_id FROM #TargetQueryIds))
    GROUP BY rs.plan_id
)
SELECT TOP (@RowCap)
    q.query_id,
    COALESCE(
        CASE WHEN q.object_id = 0 THEN NULL ELSE OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(q.object_id) + N'.' + OBJECT_NAME(q.object_id) END,
        CASE WHEN dq.object_id IS NULL OR dq.object_id = 0 THEN NULL ELSE OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(dq.object_id) + N'.' + OBJECT_NAME(dq.object_id) END
    ) AS proc_name,
    qt.query_sql_text,
    a.plan_id,
    p.is_forced_plan,
    a.count_executions,
    CAST(a.avg_duration_us / 1000.0 AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_duration_ms,
    CAST(a.max_duration_us / 1000.0 AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS max_duration_ms,
    CAST(a.min_duration_us / 1000.0 AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS min_duration_ms,
    CAST(a.avg_cpu_us / 1000.0 AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_cpu_ms,
    CAST(a.avg_logical_reads AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_logical_reads,
    CAST(a.avg_physical_reads AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_physical_reads,
    CAST(a.avg_rowcount AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_rowcount,
    a.non_regular_count,
    p.last_execution_time,
    CAST(p.query_plan AS NVARCHAR(MAX)) AS query_plan_xml,
    CASE WHEN t.dispatcher_query_id IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS is_psp_variant,
    t.dispatcher_query_id AS psp_dispatcher_query_id
FROM Agg AS a
JOIN sys.query_store_plan AS p        ON p.plan_id = a.plan_id
JOIN sys.query_store_query AS q       ON q.query_id = p.query_id
JOIN sys.query_store_query_text AS qt ON qt.query_text_id = q.query_text_id
JOIN #TargetQueryIds AS t             ON t.query_id = q.query_id
LEFT JOIN sys.query_store_query AS dq ON dq.query_id = t.dispatcher_query_id
ORDER BY a.count_executions * a.avg_duration_us DESC
OPTION (RECOMPILE);

DROP TABLE #TargetQueryIds;
'@

# Plan-cache fallback path (no Query Store). Parameters bound: @ProcName
# (nvarchar, nullable), @QueryHash (varbinary(8), nullable), @StartLocal /
# @EndLocal (datetime2), @RowCap (int). Returns TWO result sets: proc-level
# rollup, then statement-level rollup.
$script:SqlPlanCache = @'
DECLARE @DbId INT = DB_ID();
DECLARE @ObjectId INT = CASE WHEN @ProcName IS NOT NULL THEN OBJECT_ID(@ProcName) END;

IF @ProcName IS NOT NULL AND @ObjectId IS NULL
BEGIN
    RAISERROR(N'Could not resolve proc name "%s" to an object in this database.', 16, 1, @ProcName);
    RETURN;
END;

-- Procedure-level rollup: used when @ProcName is supplied, or when neither
-- filter is supplied (general "what's been running" overview).
SELECT TOP (@RowCap)
    'procedure' AS source,
    OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(ps.object_id, ps.database_id) + N'.' + OBJECT_NAME(ps.object_id, ps.database_id) AS proc_name,
    CAST(NULL AS VARCHAR(20)) AS query_hash_hex,
    CAST(NULL AS NVARCHAR(MAX)) AS query_sql_text,
    ps.execution_count,
    CAST(ps.total_worker_time  / 1000.0 / ps.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_cpu_ms,
    CAST(ps.total_elapsed_time / 1000.0 / ps.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_duration_ms,
    CAST(ps.total_logical_reads  * 1.0 / ps.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_logical_reads,
    CAST(ps.total_physical_reads * 1.0 / ps.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_physical_reads,
    ps.last_execution_time,
    ps.cached_time AS plan_cached_time,
    CAST(qp.query_plan AS NVARCHAR(MAX)) AS query_plan_xml,
    CONVERT(VARCHAR(130), ps.plan_handle, 1) AS plan_handle_hex
FROM sys.dm_exec_procedure_stats AS ps
OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_query_plan(ps.plan_handle) AS qp
WHERE ps.database_id = @DbId
  AND ps.last_execution_time BETWEEN @StartLocal AND @EndLocal
  AND (@ObjectId IS NULL OR ps.object_id = @ObjectId)
  AND @QueryHash IS NULL
ORDER BY ps.total_worker_time DESC
OPTION (RECOMPILE);

-- Statement-level rollup: used when @QueryHash is supplied, or when neither
-- filter is supplied. Server-wide (plan cache statement text is not neatly
-- scoped to one database the way Query Store is).
SELECT TOP (@RowCap)
    'statement' AS source,
    CAST(NULL AS NVARCHAR(261)) AS proc_name,
    CONVERT(VARCHAR(20), qs.query_hash, 1) AS query_hash_hex,
    st.text AS query_sql_text,
    qs.execution_count,
    CAST(qs.total_worker_time  / 1000.0 / qs.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_cpu_ms,
    CAST(qs.total_elapsed_time / 1000.0 / qs.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_duration_ms,
    CAST(qs.total_logical_reads  * 1.0 / qs.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_logical_reads,
    CAST(qs.total_physical_reads * 1.0 / qs.execution_count AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_physical_reads,
    qs.last_execution_time,
    qs.creation_time AS plan_cached_time,
    CAST(qp.query_plan AS NVARCHAR(MAX)) AS query_plan_xml,
    CONVERT(VARCHAR(130), qs.plan_handle, 1) AS plan_handle_hex
FROM sys.dm_exec_query_stats AS qs
OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text(qs.sql_handle) AS st
OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_query_plan(qs.plan_handle) AS qp
WHERE qs.last_execution_time BETWEEN @StartLocal AND @EndLocal
  AND (@QueryHash IS NULL OR qs.query_hash = @QueryHash)
  AND @ObjectId IS NULL
ORDER BY qs.total_worker_time DESC
OPTION (RECOMPILE);
'@

$script:SqlIsQueryStoreOn = @'
SELECT actual_state_desc
FROM sys.database_query_store_options;
'@

# Query Store ONLY: per-interval, per-plan detail (no cross-interval
# aggregation, unlike SqlQueryStore above) -- this is the same granularity
# Query Store's own "Tracked Queries" scatter chart plots: one point per
# plan per retention interval over time. Feeds the Timeline tab. There is
# no equivalent for the plan-cache fallback -- the plan cache is a single
# cumulative snapshot per plan, not a time series, so it cannot draw this
# chart (the Timeline tab says so rather than faking it).
#
# PSP-aware for the same reason as SqlQueryStore above: a PSP-optimized
# proc's dispatcher query_id has no runtime_stats rows at all, and its
# variant query_ids report object_id = 0, so without this expansion a PSP
# proc's timeline would come back completely empty.
$script:SqlQueryStoreTimeline = @'
DECLARE @ObjectId INT = CASE WHEN @ProcName IS NOT NULL THEN OBJECT_ID(@ProcName) END;

IF @ProcName IS NOT NULL AND @ObjectId IS NULL
BEGIN
    RAISERROR(N'Could not resolve proc name "%s" to an object in this database.', 16, 1, @ProcName);
    RETURN;
END;

CREATE TABLE #TlTargetQueryIds (query_id BIGINT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, dispatcher_query_id BIGINT NULL);

INSERT INTO #TlTargetQueryIds (query_id, dispatcher_query_id)
SELECT q.query_id, NULL
FROM sys.query_store_query AS q
WHERE (@QueryId  IS NULL OR q.query_id  = @QueryId)
  AND (@ObjectId IS NULL OR q.object_id = @ObjectId);

IF OBJECT_ID('sys.query_store_query_variant') IS NOT NULL
BEGIN
    DECLARE @TlPspSql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'
INSERT INTO #TlTargetQueryIds (query_id, dispatcher_query_id)
SELECT qv.query_variant_query_id, qv.parent_query_id
FROM sys.query_store_query_variant AS qv
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM #TlTargetQueryIds t WHERE t.query_id = qv.parent_query_id)
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM #TlTargetQueryIds t2 WHERE t2.query_id = qv.query_variant_query_id);';
    EXEC sys.sp_executesql @TlPspSql;
END;

;WITH Ivl AS (
    SELECT runtime_stats_interval_id, end_time
    FROM sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval
    WHERE start_time < @EndUtc AND end_time > @StartUtc
)
SELECT TOP (@RowCap)
    q.query_id,
    p.plan_id,
    COALESCE(
        CASE WHEN q.object_id = 0 THEN NULL ELSE OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(q.object_id) + N'.' + OBJECT_NAME(q.object_id) END,
        CASE WHEN dq.object_id IS NULL OR dq.object_id = 0 THEN NULL ELSE OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(dq.object_id) + N'.' + OBJECT_NAME(dq.object_id) END
    ) AS proc_name,
    i.end_time AS interval_end_time,
    rs.count_executions,
    CAST(rs.avg_duration / 1000.0 AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_duration_ms,
    CAST(rs.avg_cpu_time / 1000.0 AS DECIMAL(19,3)) AS avg_cpu_ms,
    CAST(rs.avg_logical_io_reads AS DECIMAL(19,1)) AS avg_logical_reads
FROM sys.query_store_runtime_stats AS rs
JOIN Ivl AS i                          ON i.runtime_stats_interval_id = rs.runtime_stats_interval_id
JOIN sys.query_store_plan AS p         ON p.plan_id = rs.plan_id
JOIN sys.query_store_query AS q        ON q.query_id = p.query_id
JOIN #TlTargetQueryIds AS t            ON t.query_id = q.query_id
LEFT JOIN sys.query_store_query AS dq  ON dq.query_id = t.dispatcher_query_id
ORDER BY i.end_time
OPTION (RECOMPILE);

DROP TABLE #TlTargetQueryIds;
'@

#endregion

#region ------------------------------ Helpers --------------------------------

$script:ToolTip = New-Object System.Windows.Forms.ToolTip

# TextBox.PlaceholderText only exists on .NET (Core) WinForms, not on the
# classic .NET Framework WinForms that ships with Windows PowerShell 5.1.
# Try it, and always also set a tooltip so the hint shows up either way.
function Set-Placeholder {
    param([System.Windows.Forms.Control]$Control, [string]$Text)
    try { $Control.PlaceholderText = $Text } catch { }
    $script:ToolTip.SetToolTip($Control, $Text)
}

function New-SqlConnectionString {
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$ServerName,
        # Optional: omit (or pass blank) to connect at the server level --
        # e.g. to list databases before you've picked one -- and SQL Server
        # falls back to the login's own default database.
        [string]$DatabaseName,
        [switch]$UseSqlAuth,
        [string]$SqlUser,
        [string]$SqlPassword
    )
    $builder = New-Object System.Data.SqlClient.SqlConnectionStringBuilder
    $builder['Server']                 = $ServerName
    if ($DatabaseName) { $builder['Database'] = $DatabaseName }
    $builder['Connect Timeout']        = 10
    $builder['TrustServerCertificate'] = $true
    $builder['Encrypt']                = $false
    $builder['Application Name']       = 'QueryExecutionHistory.ps1'
    if ($UseSqlAuth) {
        $builder['User ID']  = $SqlUser
        $builder['Password'] = $SqlPassword
    } else {
        $builder['Integrated Security'] = $true
    }
    return $builder.ConnectionString
}

# --- Search history: the "cache of proc names / query ids you've
# searched" ------------------------------------------------------------
# A small JSON file next to this script, so it persists across restarts.
# $PSScriptRoot is empty if the script is dot-sourced/pasted rather than
# run with -File, so this falls back to the current directory rather
# than failing outright.
$script:SearchHistoryPath = Join-Path -Path $(if ($PSScriptRoot) { $PSScriptRoot } else { (Get-Location).Path }) -ChildPath 'QueryExecutionHistory.history.json'
$script:MaxSearchHistory = 30

function Import-SearchHistory {
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $script:SearchHistoryPath)) { return @() }
    try {
        $raw = Get-Content -LiteralPath $script:SearchHistoryPath -Raw -ErrorAction Stop
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($raw)) { return @() }
        $items = @(ConvertFrom-Json -InputObject $raw -ErrorAction Stop)
        return @($items | Where-Object { $_ } | ForEach-Object { [string]$_ })
    }
    catch {
        # Corrupt or unreadable history file shouldn't block the app from
        # starting -- just start with an empty history.
        return @()
    }
}

function Export-SearchHistory {
    param([string[]]$History)
    try {
        ($History | ConvertTo-Json) | Set-Content -LiteralPath $script:SearchHistoryPath -Encoding UTF8 -ErrorAction Stop
    }
    catch {
        # Read-only folder, locked file, etc. -- history just won't persist
        # this run; not worth interrupting the user over.
    }
}

# Adds $Text to the front of the in-memory + on-disk history (de-duplicated,
# case-insensitive, capped at $script:MaxSearchHistory) and refreshes the
# search ComboBox's dropdown list to match.
function Add-SearchHistory {
    param([string]$Text)
    $trimmed = if ($Text) { $Text.Trim() } else { '' }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($trimmed)) { return }

    $script:SearchHistory = @($script:SearchHistory | Where-Object { $_ -and ($_ -ne $trimmed) })
    $script:SearchHistory = @($trimmed) + $script:SearchHistory
    if ($script:SearchHistory.Count -gt $script:MaxSearchHistory) {
        $script:SearchHistory = $script:SearchHistory[0..($script:MaxSearchHistory - 1)]
    }
    Export-SearchHistory -History $script:SearchHistory

    $currentText = $txtSearch.Text
    $txtSearch.Items.Clear()
    if ($script:SearchHistory.Count -gt 0) { [void]$txtSearch.Items.AddRange($script:SearchHistory) }
    $txtSearch.Text = $currentText
}

# --- Per-server database list cache -------------------------------------
# A second small JSON file next to this script: { "server1": ["db",...],
# "server2": [...] }. Lets the Database dropdown pre-fill for a server
# you've listed before without a live query, and is what a blank-Database
# Connect refreshes.
$script:DatabaseCachePath = Join-Path -Path $(if ($PSScriptRoot) { $PSScriptRoot } else { (Get-Location).Path }) -ChildPath 'QueryExecutionHistory.databases.json'

function Import-DatabaseCache {
    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $script:DatabaseCachePath)) { return @{} }
    try {
        $raw = Get-Content -LiteralPath $script:DatabaseCachePath -Raw -ErrorAction Stop
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($raw)) { return @{} }
        $obj = ConvertFrom-Json -InputObject $raw -ErrorAction Stop
        $cache = @{}
        # ConvertFrom-Json gives a PSCustomObject for a JSON object; walk its
        # properties rather than assuming it behaves like a hashtable.
        foreach ($prop in $obj.PSObject.Properties) {
            $cache[$prop.Name] = @($prop.Value | Where-Object { $_ } | ForEach-Object { [string]$_ })
        }
        return $cache
    }
    catch {
        return @{}
    }
}

function Export-DatabaseCache {
    param([hashtable]$Cache)
    try {
        ($Cache | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 3) | Set-Content -LiteralPath $script:DatabaseCachePath -Encoding UTF8 -ErrorAction Stop
    }
    catch {
        # Not worth interrupting the user over a failed cache write.
    }
}

# Resolves what the user typed into the search box:
#   pure integer      -> Query Store query_id
#   0x.... hex string -> plan-cache query_hash
#   anything else     -> proc name (schema.name); schema defaults to dbo
function Resolve-SearchInput {
    param([string]$Text)

    $result = [ordered]@{ QueryId = $null; QueryHash = $null; ProcName = $null }
    $trimmed = if ($Text) { $Text.Trim() } else { '' }
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($trimmed)) {
        return $result
    }
    if ($trimmed -match '^\d+$') {
        $result.QueryId = [int]$trimmed
    }
    elseif ($trimmed -match '^0x[0-9a-fA-F]+$') {
        $hex = $trimmed.Substring(2)
        if ($hex.Length % 2 -eq 1) { $hex = '0' + $hex }
        $bytes = New-Object byte[] ($hex.Length / 2)
        for ($i = 0; $i -lt $bytes.Length; $i++) {
            $bytes[$i] = [Convert]::ToByte($hex.Substring($i * 2, 2), 16)
        }
        $result.QueryHash = $bytes
    }
    else {
        $result.ProcName = if ($trimmed -notmatch '\.') { "dbo.$trimmed" } else { $trimmed }
    }
    return $result
}

function Get-TimeRangeUtc {
    param([string]$Preset, [Nullable[datetime]]$CustomStartLocal, [Nullable[datetime]]$CustomEndLocal)
    $nowUtc = [DateTime]::UtcNow
    switch ($Preset) {
        'Last 30 minutes' { return @{ StartUtc = $nowUtc.AddMinutes(-30); EndUtc = $nowUtc } }
        'Last 1 hour'     { return @{ StartUtc = $nowUtc.AddHours(-1);    EndUtc = $nowUtc } }
        'Last 4 hours'    { return @{ StartUtc = $nowUtc.AddHours(-4);    EndUtc = $nowUtc } }
        'Last 24 hours'   { return @{ StartUtc = $nowUtc.AddHours(-24);   EndUtc = $nowUtc } }
        'Custom'          {
            return @{ StartUtc = $CustomStartLocal.Value.ToUniversalTime(); EndUtc = $CustomEndLocal.Value.ToUniversalTime() }
        }
        default { return @{ StartUtc = $nowUtc.AddMinutes(-30); EndUtc = $nowUtc } }
    }
}

function Test-QueryStoreEnabled {
    param([string]$ConnectionString)
    $conn = New-Object System.Data.SqlClient.SqlConnection $ConnectionString
    try {
        $conn.Open()
        $cmd = $conn.CreateCommand()
        $cmd.CommandText = $script:SqlIsQueryStoreOn
        $cmd.CommandTimeout = 10
        $reader = $cmd.ExecuteReader()
        try {
            if ($reader.Read()) {
                $state = $reader['actual_state_desc']
                return ($state -and $state -ne 'OFF')
            }
            return $false   # no row in sys.database_query_store_options => QS never configured
        } finally { $reader.Close() }
    }
    catch {
        # Older compat level, permissions issue, etc. Treat as "unknown / off"
        # and let the caller fall back to the plan-cache path.
        return $false
    }
    finally { $conn.Close() }
}

# Lists ONLINE databases on the server this connection string points at
# (Database key omitted -- see New-SqlConnectionString). sys.databases is
# a server-scoped catalog view, so this works regardless of which
# database the login happens to default into.
function Get-ServerDatabases {
    param([string]$ConnectionString)
    $conn = New-Object System.Data.SqlClient.SqlConnection $ConnectionString
    try {
        $conn.Open()
        $cmd = $conn.CreateCommand()
        $cmd.CommandText = "SELECT name FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' ORDER BY name;"
        $cmd.CommandTimeout = 10
        $reader = $cmd.ExecuteReader()
        try {
            $names = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
            while ($reader.Read()) { $names.Add([string]$reader['name']) }
            return @($names)
        } finally { $reader.Close() }
    }
    finally { $conn.Close() }
}

# Builds a strongly-typed SqlParameter. We deliberately do NOT use
# Parameters.AddWithValue here: AddWithValue infers the SQL type from the
# .NET value, and a $null search value (very common here -- e.g. @QueryId
# when the user searched by proc name instead) gives it nothing to infer
# from, which can produce a parameter of the wrong SqlDbType. Always
# setting the type explicitly avoids that class of "operand type clash" /
# unexpected-implicit-conversion bug regardless of which fields are null.
function New-SqlParam {
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$Name,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [System.Data.SqlDbType]$Type,
        $Value,
        [int]$Size = 0
    )
    $p = New-Object System.Data.SqlClient.SqlParameter
    $p.ParameterName = $Name
    $p.SqlDbType = $Type
    if ($Size -gt 0) { $p.Size = $Size }
    $p.Value = if ($null -eq $Value) { [DBNull]::Value } else { $Value }
    return $p
}

# Runs $CommandText against $ConnectionString with an array of SqlParameter
# objects (build them with New-SqlParam) and returns a System.Data.DataSet
# (one table per SQL result set). Kept here for programmatic/console use;
# the GUI's async search path uses its own copy of this logic because it
# runs in an isolated runspace that does not see this script's functions.
function Invoke-HistoryQuery {
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$ConnectionString,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$CommandText,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [System.Data.SqlClient.SqlParameter[]]$Parameters,
        [int]$TimeoutSec = 30
    )
    $conn = New-Object System.Data.SqlClient.SqlConnection $ConnectionString
    $cmd  = $conn.CreateCommand()
    $cmd.CommandText    = $CommandText
    $cmd.CommandTimeout = $TimeoutSec
    [void]$cmd.Parameters.AddRange($Parameters)
    $adapter = New-Object System.Data.SqlClient.SqlDataAdapter $cmd
    $dataSet = New-Object System.Data.DataSet
    try {
        $conn.Open()
        [void]$adapter.Fill($dataSet)
    }
    finally {
        $conn.Close()
    }
    return $dataSet
}

# Runs a single non-query SQL statement (EXEC/DBCC) synchronously against
# $ConnectionString with the given parameters. Used for the Remove Plan
# feature: those calls are quick (metadata-only, like everything else in
# this tool), and doing them synchronously means the confirm-dialog ->
# run -> success/fail flow stays simple and immediate.
function Invoke-HistoryCommand {
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$ConnectionString,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)] [string]$CommandText,
        [System.Data.SqlClient.SqlParameter[]]$Parameters = @(),
        [int]$TimeoutSec = 30
    )
    $conn = New-Object System.Data.SqlClient.SqlConnection $ConnectionString
    $cmd  = $conn.CreateCommand()
    $cmd.CommandText    = $CommandText
    $cmd.CommandTimeout = $TimeoutSec
    if ($Parameters.Count -gt 0) { [void]$cmd.Parameters.AddRange($Parameters) }
    try {
        $conn.Open()
        [void]$cmd.ExecuteNonQuery()
    }
    finally {
        $conn.Close()
    }
}

